  "id": "COMPOUND:41541 - PROTEIN:4896",
  "verbalization": "Leoheterin is implicated in the regulation of the sodium/bile acid cotransporter through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Leoheterin acts as a target of various compounds, which in turn modulate the sodium/bile acid cotransporter. Specifically, Leoheterin is linked to Taste receptor type 2 member 31, which is a target of two distinct compounds—(24S)-24,25-Dihydroxysunpollenol and 14,15-Dimethoxy-20-methyl-5,7-dioxa-20-azapentacyclo[10.5.3.01,13.02,10.04,8]icosa-2,4(8),9,14-tetraene-16,19-dione—both of which are known to interact with the sodium/bile acid cotransporter. Additionally, Leoheterin is also linked to Kruppel-like factor 5, which is a target of Cercophorin B, another compound that interacts with the sodium/bile acid cotransporter. These findings collectively suggest that Leoheterin may influence the sodium/bile acid cotransporter through a series of molecular interactions, potentially via the modulation of receptor activity or signaling pathways. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, with Leoheterin serving as a central node in a network of targets that ultimately affect the sodium/bile acid cotransporter. The evidence supports a mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interactions remains to be fully elucidated.",
